];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can ravens talk like parrots?', a: 'Ravens can produce human words clearly enough to be understood — there are many documented captive ravens that use words consistently. However, ravens are not "talking" in the sense of understanding language: they are vocal mimics, like parrots, mynas, and some other birds. Wild ravens mimic other animals and environmental sounds; word mimicry occurs primarily in birds hand-raised with human contact.' },
  { q: 'Do wild ravens mimic human speech?', a: 'Wild ravens do occasionally mimic human voices or words if raised in contact with people, but this is not typical wild behaviour. More commonly, wild ravens mimic the calls of wolves, other birds, and environmental sounds — behaviour linked to the information-centre hypothesis and social manipulation.' },
  { q: 'Which is a better talker — a raven or a parrot?', a: 'African Grey Parrots and some other psittacines produce a larger vocabulary and longer phrases than ravens in controlled experiments. However, Common Ravens are exceptional vocal mimics for a non-psittacine species. Ravens also excel at mimicry of non-human sounds (wolves, wind, tools) in ways parrots typically do not.' },
];
